# What is a Paper Prototype?

<div style="background-color: #F7F8FA; border-radius: 40px; color: #000000; width: 95%; padding: 10px 10px 10px 10px;"> <p style="line-height: 1.5; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; font-size: 18px;">

## **Key Vocabulary**
* #### **Prototype** - A first or early model of a product that allows you to test assumptions before developing a final version.
* #### **User Interface** - The visual elements of a program through which a user controls or communicates with the application. Often abbreviated UI.</p> </div>
<br>

#### Throughout the Design Process unit, students will use **paper prototypes** as a quick tool to kick off the development of an app. Paper prototypes are used by designers in many industries to create low-stakes, low-cost first drafts of their final products. 

#### Check out some real-world examples below. While you do so, ask yourself, **"Why might a designer choose to start with a paper prototype?"**
<br>

<table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 20px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="background-color: #4A4A4A; color: #FFFFFF;">
      <th style="padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 35%;">Image</th>
      <th style="padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 65%;">Description</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr style="background-color: #F5F7F8;">
      <td style="padding: 15px; vertical-align: top;">
        <img src="https://images.code.org/9e9fd1f7ce74a44e7a23fd1ae3a268f2-image-1704411539273.38.56 PM.png" alt="A paper app prototype placed on top of a tablet screen." style="width: 100%; border-radius: 10px;">
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 15px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="margin-top: 0; font-size: 14pt;"><strong>Example 1</strong></p>
        <p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.4;">
          This prototype combines paper with a digital device. The designer has placed their app screens on top of an iPad to help users understand the app’s scale during testing. The design appears to recommend curated video playlists, with options to skip a video or save it for later.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 0;">
          Tobe101, 
          <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0" target="blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, 
          via Wikimedia Commons
        </p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding: 15px; vertical-align: top;">
        <img src="https://images.code.org/f13061ba5f0e7e9ffb9371759abd06e8-image-1704411638371.40.25 PM.png" alt="A cardboard mobile phone prototype with screen elements and buttons." style="width: 100%; border-radius: 10px;">
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 15px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="margin-top: 0; font-size: 14pt;"><strong>Example 2</strong></p>
        <p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.4;">
          Although it is made from cardboard, this is still a paper prototype. The designer has created a phone-sized model with a screen and buttons. You might invite learners to infer the app’s purpose based on the visible controls and layout.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 0;">
          d_jan, 
          <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0" target="blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY 2.0</a>, 
          via Wikimedia Commons
        </p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="background-color: #F5F7F8;">
      <td style="padding: 15px; vertical-align: top;">
        <img src="https://images.code.org/3b408cbe44083f9e5730038e4a439efd-image-1704411777716.42.54 PM.png" alt="Designers testing a paper prototype for a board game." style="width: 100%; border-radius: 10px;">
      </td>
      <td style="padding: 15px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="margin-top: 0; font-size: 14pt;"><strong>Example 3</strong></p>
        <p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.4;">
          Paper prototypes are not only useful for apps. In this image, designers are prototyping a board game. They appear to be role-playing gameplay in order to identify pain points and gather feedback before creating the next version of the game.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 0;">
          “<a href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/_scypher/4552934264/" target="blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">First prototype</a>” 
          by 
          <a href="https://www.flickr.com/people/_scypher/" target="blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Elliot Trinidad</a>, 
          <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/2.0/" target="blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-NC 2.0</a>
        </p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
MARKDOWN
